<%
  const helpPrefix = locals.helpIdPrefix || 'quran-memorization-help';
%>
<p class="lead fs-6">Assess each āyah once, then let daily Mudhakkir manage its learning stage and schedule from the recall grades you give. Your progress and review history stay synced across devices.</p>
<p><strong>Practice</strong> is the hide-and-reveal Mushaf view. The green <strong>Mudhakkir</strong> button starts scheduled or custom recall from the reading-mode bar, an āyah menu, or the bottom of a Practice page.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="<%= helpPrefix %>-steps">
  <h3 id="<%= helpPrefix %>-steps">A simple memorization routine</h3>
  <ol class="quran-memorization-help-steps">
    <li><span>1</span><div><strong>Assess it once in Practice</strong><p>For an āyah, page, or surah that has not been started, choose the action that honestly matches your present recall: Learn, Hard, Good, or Easy. This is the only time you select its starting stage. The āyah marker menu can also Play, Reveal, or Hide the complete āyah while you practice.</p></div></li>
    <li><span>2</span><div><strong>Recall new and due āyāt</strong><p>Start Mudhakkir for the Learning āyāt and scheduled reviews selected for the session. Learning āyāt appear first. After enrollment, your only manual actions are Pause learning, Learn later, or Easy; Mudhakkir grades handle all other stage changes.</p></div></li>
    <li><span>3</span><div><strong>Recall, reveal, then grade</strong><p>The final line of the preceding Mushaf page stays visible above the review page to support page-to-page continuity. Recite the complete āyah before selecting Reveal, then grade how independently you recalled it. Undo restores the most recent grade and previous review date. Skip leaves its stage and schedule unchanged.</p></div></li>
    <li><span>4</span><div><strong>Let Mudhakkir manage progress</strong><p>Again and Hard keep Learning or Hard āyāt in their current stage. One Easy review or two consecutive Good reviews can move them to Good once their calculated recall difficulty is no longer high. An āyah with difficulty greater than 6 remains Hard. Again on a Good āyah places it in Hard recovery. Every grade also updates its next review date.</p></div></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="<%= helpPrefix %>-fsrs">
  <h3 id="<%= helpPrefix %>-fsrs">How Mudhakkir timing adapts</h3>
  <p>The app uses FSRS 6 to estimate each āyah's difficulty and memory strength from your grades and the time between reviews. There is no fixed day-by-day ladder: Again brings an āyah back sooner, while Hard, Good, and Easy usually produce progressively longer intervals as its recall becomes stronger.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="<%= helpPrefix %>-limits">
  <h3 id="<%= helpPrefix %>-limits">What enters a Mudhakkir session</h3>
  <p>Starting Mudhakkir selects due ayat and a limited number of new Learning ayat within your session settings. Learning ayat appear first. Later, Easy, and Paused ayat do not enter regular Mudhakkir.</p>
  <p class="mt-2">When nothing is due, a new session may include upcoming active ayat. Change the session size and category limits in Quran memorization settings or review settings. The Maximum Hard āyāt setting is shared by fragile Hard āyāt and Good āyāt recovering after Again.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="<%= helpPrefix %>-surah-review">
  <h3 id="<%= helpPrefix %>-surah-review">Using Mudhakkir for an āyah, page, or surah</h3>
  <p>From an āyah dropdown in Practice, select <strong>Mudhakkir</strong> to begin with its current Study passage. Use the green Mudhakkir button in the reading-mode bar or the page Mudhakkir button to begin with the current Mushaf page, or choose <strong>Surah Mudhakkir</strong> from Memorization Progress. Every launcher uses the same choices: stop when the selected passage, page, or surah is complete, or continue forward until you end the session.</p>
  <p class="mt-2">Choose <strong>āyah by āyah</strong> to reveal and grade each āyah separately. Choose <strong>passage by passage</strong> to reveal and grade each complete Study passage as one unit. The selected grade is still recorded for every āyah in the passage, so each āyah keeps its own schedule.</p>
  <p class="mt-2">Custom reviews follow Quran order and enroll untouched āyāt as Learning only when reached. Already enrolled āyāt keep their current state and schedule. These reviews are not limited by what is due now, regular-session caps, or the time budget. Grades update the schedule; Skip leaves the āyah unchanged.</p>
  <p class="mt-2">The Progress page lists your active and paused sessions. Switching reviews automatically pauses the current session and resumes the selected one. You can end any active or paused session while preserving its completed reviews.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="<%= helpPrefix %>-states">
  <h3 id="<%= helpPrefix %>-states">Memorization states</h3>
  <p class="text-muted small">For an āyah you have not started, choose Learn, Hard, Good, or Easy as its initial self-assessment. After that, the app changes its learning state from your Mudhakkir grades. You can still choose Pause learning, Learn later, or Easy manually.</p>
  <dl class="quran-memorization-help-states">
    <div class="is-learning"><dt><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Learn</dt><dd>Starts the āyah in Learning. It enters Mudhakkir as a new card and stays there until an Easy review or two consecutive Good reviews.</dd></div>
    <div class="is-weak"><dt><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Hard</dt><dd>You can recite the āyah, but recall is fragile. An FSRS recall-difficulty score greater than 6 keeps it here, scheduled with shorter intervals. Recovery after Again on a Good āyah is also shown here.</dd></div>
    <div class="is-review"><dt><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Good</dt><dd>You can recite the complete āyah independently. It remains in scheduled Mudhakkir so the app can maintain it and adapt its timing.</dd></div>
    <div class="is-core"><dt><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Easy</dt><dd>You know the āyah by heart, so it does not enter regular Mudhakkir. It can also become Easy automatically after a successful review when its stability is greater than 730 days and difficulty is below 2.</dd></div>
    <div class="is-suspended"><dt><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Pause learning</dt><dd>Temporarily stops learning and reviews without deleting history. Its resulting status is Paused.</dd></div>
    <div class="is-later"><dt><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Learn later</dt><dd>Removes the āyah from active memorization and Mudhakkir until you are ready to begin again. Its resulting status is Later.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="<%= helpPrefix %>-grades">
  <h3 id="<%= helpPrefix %>-grades">Mudhakkir grades</h3>
  <div class="table-responsive">
    <table class="table table-sm align-middle quran-memorization-help-grades mb-0">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="is-again">Again</span></th><td>You could not complete the āyah without reading or help. It returns sooner and may appear once more near the end of this session. On a Good āyah, it begins Hard recovery.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="is-hard">Hard</span></th><td>You recalled it independently with significant hesitation. It stays in Learning or Hard when applicable and receives a shorter interval.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="is-good">Good</span></th><td>You recalled it with ordinary effort. It usually receives a longer interval; two consecutive Good reviews can move Learning or Hard to Good once calculated difficulty is 6 or lower.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="is-easy">Easy</span></th><td>Recall was fluent and effortless. It usually receives the longest interval and can move Learning or Hard to Good once calculated difficulty is 6 or lower.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="is-skip">Skip</span></th><td>Move on without grading the āyah or changing its learning stage or schedule.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="<%= helpPrefix %>-settings">
  <h3 id="<%= helpPrefix %>-settings">Mudhakkir settings explained</h3>
  <p>These settings control when reviews return and how many āyāt enter a regular scheduled session. Custom reviews started from an āyah, page, or surah deliberately ignore regular-session limits so you can complete the selected scope.</p>

  <h4 class="fs-6 mt-3">Mudhakkir timing</h4>
  <dl>
    <dt>Target Memory Goal</dt><dd>The percentage of scheduled āyāt you aim to recall successfully. Raising it shortens intervals and can increase Due now; lowering it permits longer intervals. Changing it immediately recalculates every active, initialized review date without adding artificial review history.</dd>
    <dt>Initial Memory Strength</dt><dd>Sets the starting stability and first intervals for newly assessed Hard, Good, or Easy āyāt. It does not rewrite the history or current strength of cards you have already reviewed.</dd>
    <dt>Mudhakkir Interval Multiplier</dt><dd>Controls how quickly stability and intervals grow after successful reviews. Conservative returns successful āyāt sooner, Standard provides the normal balance, and Aggressive spaces them farther apart.</dd>
    <dt>Lapse Recovery Speed</dt><dd>Controls how much established memory strength is retained after Again. Strict shortens recovery intervals more sharply, Standard uses the normal penalty, and Mild preserves more previous strength.</dd>
    <dt>Personalize Mudhakkir Timing</dt><dd>Analyzes at least 100 of your graded reviews and tunes the underlying timing parameters to your recall history. Run it occasionally as your history grows; the understandable controls above remain available.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h4 class="fs-6 mt-3">Mudhakkir session</h4>
  <dl>
    <dt>Maximum āyāt per session</dt><dd>The overall limit for one regular review session. Category limits below operate inside this maximum, so the session never exceeds it.</dd>
    <dt>Optional time budget</dt><dd>Stops admitting more work after the configured number of minutes. Set it to zero when you do not want a time limit.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h4 class="fs-6 mt-3">Advanced session limits</h4>
  <dl>
    <dt>Maximum Learning āyāt</dt><dd>Limits newly enrolled and still-learning āyāt admitted to one regular session. Learning appears first, but overdue Learning precedes brand-new Learning.</dd>
    <dt>Maximum Hard āyāt</dt><dd>Sets one combined limit for fragile scheduled āyāt and previously Good āyāt recovering after Again.</dd>
    <dt>Maximum Good āyāt</dt><dd>Limits established Good āyāt admitted for maintenance review.</dd>
    <dt>Order within each queue</dt><dd>Oldest due first prioritizes the most overdue work, Quran order follows surah and āyah sequence, and Random shuffles cards within each category. Learning, Hard recovery, fragile Hard, and Good priority still applies, while both Hard queues share the same maximum.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p class="text-muted small mb-0">Due now is the complete eligible backlog, not the size of the next session. A session can therefore finish while more āyāt remain due because the overall limit, category limits, or time budget was reached.</p>
</section>
